export const W1={title:'What is health? Equity, equality and the determinants of health',hook:'Two children live five miles apart. One has a pediatrician, a park and a grocery store nearby; the other has an emergency room, a highway and a convenience store. Their health will likely differ long before either makes a choice about diet or exercise. Understanding why begins with defining health and the forces that shape it.',objectives:['State and interpret the WHO definition of health.','Distinguish health differences, disparities, inequities, equality and equity.','Describe the social determinants of health and the five Healthy People 2030 domains.','Explain how structural determinants shape the distribution of health.'],sections:[
['Defining health',[
'The Constitution of the World Health Organization, adopted in 1946 and in force since 1948, defines health as “a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity.” The definition was ambitious: it placed mental and social well-being alongside physical health and rejected the idea that health simply means not being sick. The same constitution declares that the highest attainable standard of health is a fundamental right of every human being, without distinction of race, religion, political belief, economic or social condition. Critics note that “complete” well-being is rarely achieved, and some propose defining health as the ability to adapt and self-manage in the face of challenges. Both views emphasize that health is broader than medical care.',
'Health can be described for individuals and for **populations**. Population health looks at the distribution of health outcomes within a group and the factors that produce that distribution. A population perspective reveals patterns that individual cases hide, such as differences in life expectancy between neighborhoods or countries.']],
['Differences, disparities and inequities',[
'Not every difference in health is unjust. Older people have more chronic disease than younger people, a difference largely driven by biology. A **health disparity** is a particular type of health difference closely linked with social, economic or environmental disadvantage, affecting groups who have systematically experienced greater obstacles to health. WHO describes **health equity** as the absence of unfair, avoidable or remediable differences among groups of people, whether those groups are defined socially, economically, demographically, geographically, or by sex, gender, ethnicity, disability or sexual orientation. **Health inequities** are differences that are unfair and avoidable. WHO states that health equity is achieved when everyone can reach their full potential for health and well-being.',
'**Equality** means giving everyone the same resources; **equity** means giving people what they need to reach the same opportunity for health. A clinic open only during weekday working hours treats everyone equally, but workers who cannot take time off have less real access. An equitable clinic might add evening hours or mobile services. Equity sometimes requires unequal distribution of resources to correct unequal starting points.',
sort('Equality or equity?',['Equality (same for everyone)','Equity (according to need)'],[['Every school receives the same number of nurses',0],['Every patient receives the same brochure in English',0],['A clinic adds interpreters for patients with limited English',1],['Extra outreach to neighborhoods with low vaccination rates',1],['Every family gets the same bus pass',0],['Transportation vouchers for patients who live far from care',1]])]],
['The social determinants of health',[
'WHO describes the **social determinants of health** as the conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work, play and age, along with the wider forces that shape those conditions. Healthy People 2030, the U.S. national health objectives, groups them into five domains: **economic stability** (employment, income, food and housing security), **education access and quality** (early childhood education, literacy, high school graduation), **health care access and quality** (insurance, a usual source of care, health literacy), **neighborhood and built environment** (housing quality, transportation, safety, air and water quality) and **social and community context** (social support, civic participation, discrimination). Researchers estimate that medical care accounts for only a portion of variation in health outcomes, with social, economic, environmental and behavioral factors accounting for much of the rest.',
match('Match each example to its Healthy People 2030 domain',[['A family spends half its income on rent','Economic stability'],['A child attends an underfunded school','Education access and quality'],['A town has no primary care clinic','Health care access and quality'],['A neighborhood lacks sidewalks and grocery stores','Neighborhood and built environment'],['An older adult has no one to call in an emergency','Social and community context']])]],
['Structural determinants and power',[
'WHO also highlights **structural determinants**—political, legal and economic systems, social norms and institutional processes—that shape how power and resources are distributed. Discrimination, stereotyping and prejudice based on sex, gender, age, race, ethnicity or disability can worsen living conditions and can be built into institutions, leaving some groups underrepresented in decision-making and underserved. Historical policies, such as residential segregation and discriminatory lending, shaped where people live, which schools they attend and what environmental hazards they face, with effects that persist for generations.',
'A useful image is a stream: medical care pulls people out of the river downstream, while upstream factors determine who falls in. **Upstream** interventions change conditions, such as housing policy or minimum wages; **midstream** interventions address living and working conditions or behaviors; **downstream** interventions treat disease. Reducing disparities usually requires action at every level.',
sort('Upstream, midstream or downstream?',['Upstream: policies and systems','Midstream: living conditions and behaviors','Downstream: medical treatment'],[['Raising the minimum wage',0],['Banning discriminatory lending',0],['Workplace smoking-cessation program',1],['Fixing mold in apartments',1],['Insulin for a patient with diabetes',2],['Surgery after a heart attack',2]])]],
['Apply the concepts',[
'Return to the two children. Their likely health differences arise from economic stability, neighborhood environment and access to care, not from choices they have made. Those differences become **disparities** because they follow social disadvantage, and **inequities** because they are avoidable. An equitable response might include a school-based clinic, safer play spaces, healthy food access and policies that reduce neighborhood segregation. On the HOSA test, look for questions that ask you to classify a difference, name a determinant domain or identify an equity-based solution.',
flip('Key definitions',[['Health (WHO)','Complete physical, mental and social well-being, not merely absence of disease'],['Health disparity','A health difference closely linked with social, economic or environmental disadvantage'],['Health equity','Absence of unfair, avoidable or remediable differences among groups'],['Social determinants','Conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work, play and age'],['Structural determinants','Political, legal and economic systems that distribute power and resources'],['Equity vs equality','Equity gives according to need; equality gives the same to all']])]],
['Models of health',[
"Different models explain health in different ways. The **biomedical model** focuses on disease, biology and treatment; it has produced vaccines, antibiotics and surgery but can overlook social causes. The **biopsychosocial model**, proposed by psychiatrist George Engel in 1977, adds psychological and social factors, recognizing that stress, relationships and beliefs affect illness and recovery. The **social model of health** emphasizes the social, economic and environmental conditions that shape health across populations. A widely used illustration, the Dahlgren–Whitehead “rainbow” model, places individual factors such as age, sex and heredity at the center, surrounded by layers of individual lifestyle, social and community networks, living and working conditions (education, work, housing, water, sanitation, health care) and, outermost, general socioeconomic, cultural and environmental conditions.",
"These models do not compete so much as complement each other. Treating a child’s asthma requires inhaled medicines (biomedical), attention to stress and family routines (biopsychosocial) and action on housing mold and air pollution (social). The HOSA test plan’s first domain, What is Health, asks you to recognize these perspectives and connect them to equity. When analyzing a disparity, a strong answer moves across the layers rather than stopping at individual behavior.",
{t:'table',caption:"Comparing models of health",head:["Model","Focus","Example action"],rows:[["Biomedical","Disease and biology","Prescribe medication"],["Biopsychosocial","Biology, mind and relationships","Address stress and family support"],["Social","Living conditions and structures","Improve housing and air quality"]]}]]
],takeaways:['WHO defines health as complete physical, mental and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease.','A disparity is a difference linked to disadvantage; equity means removing unfair, avoidable differences.','Healthy People 2030 groups social determinants into five domains; structural determinants shape them all.'],terms:[['Health equity','Absence of unfair, avoidable or remediable differences in health among groups.'],['Social determinants of health','Conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work, play and age.'],['Upstream intervention','An action that changes underlying policies or conditions.']],mcqs:[q('The WHO Constitution defines health as:','A state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity','The absence of disease','Access to a physician','Physical fitness alone','This is the 1946 WHO definition.'),q('A health disparity is best described as a health difference that:','Is closely linked with social, economic or environmental disadvantage','Is caused only by genetics','Exists only between countries','Cannot be measured','Disparities follow disadvantage.'),q('Which describes equity rather than equality?','Providing interpreters for patients with limited English','Giving every patient the same English brochure','Opening clinics only on weekdays for all','Giving everyone the same bus pass','Equity responds to need.'),q('Which is NOT one of the five Healthy People 2030 SDOH domains?','Genetic inheritance','Economic stability','Neighborhood and built environment','Social and community context','The five domains are social and environmental.'),q('According to WHO, health equity is achieved when:','Everyone can reach their full potential for health and well-being','Everyone receives identical services','Hospitals are equally funded','Life expectancy is the same everywhere','This is WHO’s description.'),q('Raising the minimum wage to improve health is an example of an intervention that is:','Upstream','Downstream','Midstream only','Clinical','It changes underlying conditions.'),q('Structural determinants include:','Political, legal and economic systems that distribute power and resources','Individual blood pressure readings','Hospital bed counts only','A patient’s favorite food','They shape social determinants.'),q('A difference in chronic disease between 20-year-olds and 80-year-olds is mainly:','A health difference driven largely by age, not necessarily a disparity','A health inequity','Proof of discrimination','A structural determinant','Not every difference is a disparity.')],frq:{prompt:'Two neighborhoods in the same city differ in life expectancy by several years. Explain how the WHO definition of health and the social determinants of health help explain this, distinguish disparity from inequity, and propose one upstream and one downstream response.',rubric:[['Use the WHO definition and population view',['well-being','physical','mental','social','population']],['Identify social determinants by domain',['economic','neighborhood','education','access','social']],['Distinguish disparity and inequity',['disparity','inequity','avoidable','unfair','disadvantage']],['Propose upstream and downstream responses',['upstream','downstream','policy','housing','clinic','treatment']]],model:'The WHO definition treats health as complete physical, mental and social well-being, so differences in life expectancy reflect more than access to doctors. Neighborhoods differ in economic stability, housing quality, safety, schools, food access and health care, the Healthy People 2030 domains of social determinants, and structural factors such as past segregation shaped these conditions. The life expectancy gap is a disparity because it follows social and economic disadvantage, and an inequity because it is unfair and avoidable. An upstream response could be affordable housing and anti-discrimination policy; a downstream response could be a community health center offering chronic disease care in the lower-life-expectancy neighborhood.'},sources:[RULES,WHO_CONST,WHO_EQ,HP_SDOH]};

export const W2={title:'Measuring health around the world',hook:'A headline says one country’s infant mortality is twice another’s. Before deciding what that means, you need to know what was counted, per how many people, in what year and how the data were collected. Population health is measured with rates, and reading them correctly is a core skill for this event.',objectives:['Define life expectancy, infant, under-5 and maternal mortality, and DALYs.','Calculate and compare rates rather than counts.','Read global data charts and identify limits of comparisons.','Describe major global trends and gaps reported by WHO, KFF and Our World in Data.'],sections:[
['The core measures',[
'**Life expectancy at birth** is the average number of years a newborn would live if current death rates by age stayed the same. It summarizes mortality across all ages; it is not a prediction for any individual. **Infant mortality rate** is deaths of infants under one year per 1,000 live births. **Under-5 mortality rate** is deaths before age five per 1,000 live births. **Maternal mortality ratio** is maternal deaths per 100,000 live births. Because infant and child deaths remove many potential years of life, they strongly affect life expectancy. A **disability-adjusted life year (DALY)** combines years of life lost to early death and years lived with disability, allowing comparisons of the total burden of different diseases.',
'Measures must be read with their units. A rate “per 1,000 live births” cannot be compared directly with one “per 100,000 population.” Age-adjusted rates remove the effect of different age structures, which matters when comparing an older population with a younger one: a country with many older people will have more deaths from heart disease even if each age group is equally healthy.',
match('Match each measure to its definition',[['Life expectancy at birth','Average years a newborn would live at current death rates'],['Infant mortality rate','Deaths under age one per 1,000 live births'],['Under-5 mortality rate','Deaths before age five per 1,000 live births'],['Maternal mortality ratio','Maternal deaths per 100,000 live births'],['DALY','Years of healthy life lost to early death and disability'],['Age-adjusted rate','Rate standardized to remove differences in age structure']])]],
['Rates, not counts',[
'Comparing counts misleads when populations differ in size. If an invented City A has 40 asthma hospitalizations among 2,000 children and City B has 60 among 6,000, City B has more cases but City A has the higher rate: 20 per 1,000 versus 10 per 1,000. Rates also allow two kinds of comparison. The **rate difference** (absolute gap) is 10 per 1,000; the **rate ratio** (relative gap) is 2. A small absolute gap can be a large relative gap when rates are low, and the reverse. Good reporting gives both. Use the population comparison laboratory to see how changing denominators changes the conclusion.',
{t:'w',kind:'equitylab',title:'Compare rates in two populations'}]],
['Reading global data',[
'Our World in Data, one of the event references, compiles long-term global data in interactive charts. Over the past two centuries, life expectancy has risen dramatically worldwide, and child mortality has fallen: Our World in Data estimates that around 1800 roughly four in ten children died before age five, compared with around 4% today. Progress has been uneven. WHO reports that life expectancy in low-income countries is about 16 years lower than in high-income countries, and that under-5 mortality among children from the poorest households is about double that of children from the richest households.',
'When reading a chart, check the title, the measure and unit, the years, the source, whether values are estimates and whether countries are grouped by income or region. Averages hide variation within countries. A national life expectancy can conceal differences of many years between regions, income groups and racial and ethnic groups. Data quality also varies: countries with weak vital registration systems rely on surveys and models, so estimates carry uncertainty.',
order('Read a data chart carefully',['Read the title and the exact measure','Check the unit and denominator','Check the years and the source','Note whether values are estimates or registered counts','Compare the groups shown and their sizes','State a conclusion with appropriate limits'])]],
['The United States compared with peers',[
'The Peterson-KFF Health System Tracker compares the United States with other large, wealthy countries. U.S. life expectancy reached 79.0 years in 2024, while comparable countries averaged 82.7 years. The United States also spends far more on health care per person—about $14,775 in 2024, nearly $5,000 more than the next highest spender, Switzerland. In 1980, the United States and peer countries had similar life expectancy and spending, but the trends diverged. The Tracker notes that life expectancy reflects factors beyond the health system, such as income inequality and social conditions. In infant mortality, the United States also ranks below many peers; countries such as Japan have among the lowest infant mortality rates in the world.',
bench('Is the conclusion supported by the data?',[{text:'Invented data: Country X spends twice as much per person on health care as Country Y, but life expectancy is two years lower in Country X.',claim:'Spending more on health care always lowers life expectancy.',supported:false,why:'One comparison cannot establish that relationship; many factors outside health spending affect life expectancy.'},{text:'Invented data: Region A reports 30 infant deaths per 1,000 live births and Region B reports 10 per 1,000.',claim:'Region A’s infant mortality rate is three times Region B’s.',supported:true,why:'30 ÷ 10 = 3; both are rates per 1,000 live births.'},{text:'Invented data: City M had 500 deaths and City N had 300 deaths last year.',claim:'City M has a higher death rate than City N.',supported:false,why:'Counts without population denominators cannot establish rates.'}])]],
['Using measures responsibly',[
'Numbers describe groups, not individuals. A higher rate in a group does not mean every member is at risk, and it does not by itself explain why the difference exists. Disaggregating data—breaking broad categories into smaller groups—can reveal hidden disparities, such as differences among Asian American subgroups that a single “Asian” category conceals. Responsible interpretation names the population, measure, year and source; distinguishes absolute and relative differences; and avoids stereotyping. In HOSA questions, read whether the question asks for a rate, a ratio, a trend or the reason behind a trend.']],
['Global trends and the epidemiological transition',[
"As countries develop, they typically pass through an **epidemiological transition**: deaths from infectious diseases, malnutrition and maternal and neonatal conditions decline, life expectancy rises, and chronic noncommunicable diseases such as heart disease, cancer, diabetes and chronic lung disease become the leading causes of death. Population aging accompanies this shift. Our World in Data charts show that global life expectancy has roughly doubled since the early twentieth century, driven by vaccines, clean water, sanitation, better nutrition, antibiotics and maternal care. Maternal mortality has also fallen substantially worldwide, though most maternal deaths still occur in low- and lower-middle-income countries, many from preventable causes such as hemorrhage, hypertensive disorders and infection.",
"Progress can stall or reverse. Conflict, economic crises, pandemics and weak health systems can erase gains, and some high-income countries have seen slower improvement in life expectancy because of chronic disease, drug overdoses and other causes. Reading trends therefore requires attention to time periods and to the causes behind them. Exam questions may ask which factors explain long-term improvements or why progress differs between regions; answers should combine medical advances with social and economic development.",
{t:'table',caption:"Typical patterns across the transition",head:["Stage","Leading causes of death","Typical life expectancy pattern"],rows:[["Early","Infections, malnutrition, maternal and neonatal conditions","Low, with high child mortality"],["Middle","Mix of infectious and chronic disease (double burden)","Rising"],["Late","Heart disease, cancer, dementia, chronic lung disease","High, with an aging population"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Use rates with denominators; infant mortality is per 1,000 live births and maternal mortality per 100,000 live births.','Report both absolute and relative differences; age-adjust when age structures differ.','The U.S. had life expectancy of 79.0 years in 2024 versus a peer average of 82.7, while spending far more per person.'],terms:[['Life expectancy','Average years a newborn would live at current age-specific death rates.'],['DALY','Disability-adjusted life year; a measure of total disease burden.'],['Disaggregation','Breaking broad data categories into smaller groups.']],mcqs:[q('Infant mortality rate is expressed as deaths under age one per:','1,000 live births','100 people','100,000 population','10,000 births','This is the standard denominator.'),q('Maternal mortality ratio is expressed per:','100,000 live births','1,000 women','100 pregnancies','10,000 people','Maternal deaths are rare, so a larger denominator is used.'),q('City A has 40 cases among 2,000 children; City B has 60 among 6,000. Which statement is correct?','City A has the higher rate','City B has the higher rate because it has more cases','The rates are equal','Rates cannot be compared','20 per 1,000 versus 10 per 1,000.'),q('According to WHO, life expectancy in low-income countries is about how many years lower than in high-income countries?','16 years','2 years','30 years','5 years','WHO reports a gap of about 16 years.'),q('According to the Peterson-KFF Health System Tracker, U.S. life expectancy in 2024 was:','79.0 years, below the peer average of 82.7','85 years, above peers','72 years','Equal to peer countries','The U.S. trails peers while spending more.'),q('Which country has among the lowest infant mortality rates in the world?','Japan','United States','Canada','Switzerland','Japan’s infant mortality is among the world’s lowest.'),q('Age adjustment is used to:','Remove the effect of different age structures when comparing populations','Make rates larger','Count only older adults','Replace denominators','It allows fair comparisons.'),q('A DALY combines:','Years of life lost to early death and years lived with disability','Hospital admissions and costs','Births and deaths','Only disability years','It measures total disease burden.')],frq:{prompt:'A report states that Region A has more infant deaths than Region B, so its infants are less healthy. Explain what additional information you need, how to calculate and compare rates, and what other limits apply when interpreting global health data.',rubric:[['Need denominators and rates',['live births','denominator','rate','per 1,000']],['Absolute and relative comparisons',['difference','ratio','absolute','relative']],['Data quality and context limits',['year','source','estimate','registration','age']],['Responsible interpretation',['cause','disaggregat','individual','stereotyp']]],model:'Counts of infant deaths are not enough; I need the number of live births in each region to calculate infant mortality rates per 1,000 live births. I would compare both the rate difference, which shows the absolute gap, and the rate ratio, which shows the relative gap. I would check that the data come from the same years and comparable sources, whether values are registered counts or survey-based estimates, and whether the regions differ in reporting quality. Even with accurate rates, the data describe groups rather than individuals and do not by themselves explain why the difference exists, so I would avoid stereotyping and look for disaggregated data and contextual factors.'},sources:[RULES,OWID_LE,OWID_CM,KFF_PEER]};

export const W3={title:'Racial and ethnic disparities in the United States',hook:'In 2022, an infant born to a Black family in the United States was more than twice as likely to die before the first birthday as an infant born to a White family. Statistics like this appear throughout the World Health & Disparities references. Understanding them requires accurate numbers, careful language and an explanation that looks beyond individual behavior to history and structure.',objectives:['Describe major U.S. racial and ethnic disparities in life expectancy, infant and maternal mortality and chronic disease.','Explain how structural racism and social determinants contribute to disparities.','Use precise, respectful language about race, ethnicity and disparities.','Recognize the importance of disaggregated data.'],sections:[
['What the data show',[
'KFF compiles federal data showing persistent disparities. In 2022, the infant mortality rate was 10.9 per 1,000 live births for Black infants compared with 4.5 for White infants; rates for American Indian and Alaska Native (9.1) and Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islander (8.5) infants were roughly twice the White rate, while the Hispanic rate was 4.9. Provisional 2022 data showed life expectancy of 72.8 years for Black people and 67.9 years for American Indian and Alaska Native people, compared with 77.5 years for White people. Diabetes death rates in 2022 were higher among Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islander, American Indian and Alaska Native, Black and Hispanic people than among White people. Pregnancy-related mortality from 2017–2019 was highest among Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islander, Black and American Indian and Alaska Native women.',
'Disparities also appear in health care. KFF reports that nonelderly American Indian and Alaska Native, Black, Hispanic and Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islander people are more likely to be uninsured than White people, despite large coverage gains after the Affordable Care Act. Disparities exist across many dimensions, not only race and ethnicity: socioeconomic status, geography, language, gender, disability, citizenship and sexual orientation.',
{t:'w',kind:'equitylab',title:'Explore rate gaps with invented data'}]],
['Why disparities exist',[
'KFF and other sources attribute these disparities to longstanding structural and systemic inequities rooted in historical and contemporary racism and discrimination. **Structural racism** refers to the ways that policies, practices and institutions—housing, education, employment, criminal justice and health care—produce unequal opportunities and outcomes by race, even without individual intent. Historical practices such as segregation and **redlining** (denying mortgages in neighborhoods with many Black residents) shaped residential patterns that still influence exposure to pollution, school quality and access to healthy food and care.',
'Race is a social category, not a precise biological one. Genetic variation within racial groups is much greater than variation between them. Using race as a proxy for biology can mislead, while recording race and ethnicity remains essential for measuring and addressing disparities caused by racism. The **weathering hypothesis** proposes that chronic stress from discrimination and disadvantage accelerates biological aging, which may help explain why some disparities persist even at higher incomes.',
sort('Individual-level or structural explanation?',['Individual-level factor','Structural factor'],[['A person’s smoking habit',0],['A patient’s missed medication doses',0],['Historical redlining of neighborhoods',1],['Underfunded schools in segregated districts',1],['Hospitals closing in majority-minority neighborhoods',1],['A person’s exercise routine',0]])]],
['Maternal and infant health',[
'Maternal and infant health disparities receive special attention because they are large and often preventable. Research has found that disparities in maternal outcomes persist across education and income levels: a Black woman with a college degree may face higher pregnancy-related risk than a White woman without one. Contributing factors include higher rates of chronic conditions such as hypertension, differences in quality of care at the hospitals where women give birth, gaps in insurance after pregnancy, and experiences of not being heard by clinicians. Many states have extended postpartum Medicaid coverage to twelve months to reduce gaps after delivery.',
bench('Is the claim supported?',[{text:'KFF reports that in 2022 the infant mortality rate was 10.9 per 1,000 live births for Black infants and 4.5 for White infants.',claim:'The Black infant mortality rate was more than twice the White rate in 2022.',supported:true,why:'10.9 ÷ 4.5 ≈ 2.4.'},{text:'KFF reports that disparities are rooted in structural inequities and occur across many dimensions, including income and geography.',claim:'Racial disparities in health are explained entirely by individual behavior.',supported:false,why:'The sources emphasize structural and social factors, not individual behavior alone.'}])]],
['Language and data',[
'Use precise, respectful language. Name the population and measure (“the infant mortality rate among Black infants”) rather than describing a group as “unhealthy.” Prefer person-first or community-preferred terms and the categories used by the data source. Federal data often use categories such as American Indian or Alaska Native, Asian, Black or African American, Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, White, and Hispanic or Latino ethnicity. **Disaggregated data** matter because broad categories can hide large differences: health outcomes vary substantially among Asian American subgroups and among Hispanic subgroups by country of origin. Small populations, such as Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islander communities, are often missing from reports because samples are too small, which can make their disparities invisible.',
match('Match the term',[['Policies and institutions that produce unequal outcomes by race','Structural racism'],['Historical denial of mortgages in certain neighborhoods','Redlining'],['Chronic stress accelerating biological aging','Weathering'],['Breaking broad categories into subgroups','Disaggregation'],['Federal ethnicity category separate from race','Hispanic or Latino']])]],
['From description to action',[
'Describing a disparity is the first step; explaining it and acting on it come next. A strong analysis states the measure and data source, identifies contributing social and structural factors, avoids blaming individuals or groups, and proposes solutions at several levels. Later lessons address bias in care and strategies to reduce disparities. On the test, expect questions asking which group has the highest rate on a given measure according to a cited source, and questions asking why disparities persist.']],
['Patterns that complicate simple explanations',[
"Not every disparity follows income in a straight line. Researchers have described a **Hispanic health paradox**: in many U.S. data sets, Hispanic people have life expectancy similar to or higher than non-Hispanic White people despite, on average, lower incomes and higher uninsured rates. Explanations under study include the health of recent immigrants, social and family support, and data issues such as return migration. The paradox is a reminder that culture, migration and social networks interact with economic factors, and that outcomes for Hispanic people vary widely by country of origin and generation.",
"Similarly, the broad “Asian” category can hide large differences: some Asian American subgroups have high rates of certain cancers, hepatitis B or diabetes at lower body weights, while others have high average incomes and long life expectancy. American Indian and Alaska Native communities experience some of the largest disparities in the nation, linked to historical displacement, underfunding of the Indian Health Service and rural isolation, yet tribal nations also lead innovative, community-designed health programs. A responsible analysis avoids treating any group as uniform and pays attention to history, place and self-determination.",
"Finally, disparities change over time. During the COVID-19 pandemic, life expectancy fell more steeply for Hispanic, Black and American Indian and Alaska Native people than for White people, widening gaps that had been narrowing. Recovery after the pandemic has been uneven. When you cite a figure, always include its year, because the same comparison can look different a few years earlier or later."]]
],takeaways:['In 2022, Black infant mortality (10.9 per 1,000) was more than twice the White rate (4.5); AIAN life expectancy was 67.9 years.','Structural racism, segregation and redlining shape social determinants; race is a social category.','Use precise language, cite data sources and seek disaggregated data.'],terms:[['Structural racism','Policies, practices and institutions that produce unequal outcomes by race.'],['Redlining','Historical denial of mortgages and services in neighborhoods with many Black residents.'],['Weathering','Hypothesis that chronic stress from disadvantage accelerates biological aging.']],mcqs:[q('According to KFF, the 2022 infant mortality rate for Black infants was:','10.9 per 1,000 live births','4.5 per 1,000 live births','1.9 per 1,000 live births','25 per 1,000 live births','The White rate was 4.5.'),q('Which group had the lowest provisional 2022 life expectancy in KFF data?','American Indian and Alaska Native people','White people','Hispanic people','Asian people','AIAN life expectancy was 67.9 years.'),q('Structural racism refers to:','Policies and institutions producing unequal outcomes by race','Only individual prejudice','Genetic differences between races','A medical diagnosis','It operates through systems.'),q('Redlining historically involved:','Denying mortgages in neighborhoods with many Black residents','Painting hospital lines red','A type of triage','Emergency room overcrowding','It shaped residential segregation.'),q('Why are disaggregated data important?','Broad categories can hide large subgroup differences','They make reports shorter','They remove the need for denominators','They prevent all bias','Subgroups can differ greatly.'),q('Which statement about race is most accurate?','Race is a social category; within-group genetic variation exceeds between-group variation','Race precisely defines biological differences','Race should never be recorded','Race explains all disparities','Recording race helps measure racism’s effects.'),q('The weathering hypothesis proposes that:','Chronic stress from disadvantage accelerates biological aging','Weather causes disparities','Older people are healthier','Disparities disappear with age','It links stress and health.'),q('According to KFF, health disparities occur across:','Race, ethnicity, income, geography, language, gender, disability and other dimensions','Race only','Income only','Only between countries','Disparities are multidimensional.')],frq:{prompt:'Using KFF data on infant mortality and life expectancy, describe two racial and ethnic disparities in the United States, explain how structural factors contribute to them, and explain why language and disaggregated data matter when discussing them.',rubric:[['Accurate data with source and year',['10.9','4.5','72.8','67.9','77.5','kff','2022']],['Structural explanation',['structural','racism','segregation','redlining','determinant']],['Avoid individual blame; race as social category',['social category','not biological','blame','individual']],['Language and disaggregation',['disaggregat','subgroup','language','precise']]],model:'KFF reports that in 2022 the infant mortality rate was 10.9 per 1,000 live births for Black infants compared with 4.5 for White infants, and that provisional 2022 life expectancy was 67.9 years for American Indian and Alaska Native people and 72.8 years for Black people compared with 77.5 for White people. These gaps reflect structural factors such as historical segregation and redlining, unequal schools, environmental exposures, gaps in insurance and differences in quality of care, rather than individual behavior alone; race is a social category, not a precise biological one. Precise language names the measure and population without labeling groups as unhealthy, and disaggregated data reveal subgroup differences that broad categories hide, including in small populations often left out of reports.'},sources:[RULES,KFF_DISP,NIMHD]};

export const W4={title:'Income, education, insurance and access to care',hook:'In 2024, 26.7 million people under age 65 in the United States had no health insurance, and most of them said coverage was simply unaffordable. Income and education shape health directly and through access to care. These economic gradients appear in every country and every reference for this event.',objectives:['Explain the socioeconomic gradient in health.','Describe how education and income influence health outcomes.','Summarize KFF data on the uninsured, including reasons and consequences.','Explain how insurance status and Medicaid expansion affect access to care.'],sections:[
['The socioeconomic gradient',[
'Health follows a **gradient**: at each step up the income or education ladder, health tends to improve, not only between the poorest and everyone else. People with higher incomes live longer on average, have lower rates of many chronic diseases and report better health. Income affects health through material resources (food, housing, safe neighborhoods), stress, time and control over work conditions, and access to care. Education affects health through employment and income, health literacy, social networks and sense of control. These factors build on one another across a lifetime, and disadvantage in childhood can shape health decades later.',
'**Socioeconomic status (SES)** combines income, education and occupation. Because these overlap, researchers often study them together. The gradient also appears in children: under-5 mortality among the poorest households is about double that among the richest, according to WHO.']],
['Who is uninsured',[
'KFF reports that in 2024, 26.7 million people under age 65 were uninsured, an uninsured rate of 9.8%. That was up from 25.3 million (9.5%) in 2023—the first increase since 2019—driven by declines in Medicaid coverage. Uninsured rates were highest among American Indian or Alaska Native people (18.9%) and Hispanic people (18.4%), compared with 6.8% for White people, 10.1% for Black people and 5.7% for Asian people. People of color made up 63.7% of the uninsured. People with incomes below twice the federal poverty level had an uninsured rate of 16.5%, compared with 4.5% above 400% of poverty. Adults aged 19–64 were nearly twice as likely as children to be uninsured.',
'Asked why they lacked coverage, 61.7% of uninsured adults said it was not affordable, 28.9% said they were not eligible and 21.0% reported difficulty signing up. Among uninsured workers, 60.5% worked for employers that did not offer insurance.',
sort('Higher or lower uninsured rate (KFF 2024)?',['Higher uninsured rate','Lower uninsured rate'],[['American Indian or Alaska Native people',0],['Hispanic people',0],['People below 200% of the federal poverty level',0],['People above 400% of the federal poverty level',1],['Children compared with working-age adults',1],['People living in Medicaid expansion states',1]])]],
['Consequences of being uninsured',[
'Lack of insurance changes how people use care. KFF reports that in 2024, 38.6% of uninsured adults delayed or skipped needed care because of cost, compared with 17.0% of privately insured adults, and 46.2% of uninsured adults did not see a doctor in the past year, compared with 14.7% of privately insured adults. Uninsured adults were also more likely to report problems paying medical bills and to carry health care debt. Delayed care can mean diagnosis at a later stage, more emergency visits and worse outcomes, while medical debt can push families further into poverty.',
bench('Is the claim supported by the KFF data?',[{text:'KFF: 61.7% of uninsured adults said coverage was not affordable; 28.9% said they were not eligible.',claim:'Cost was the most commonly reported reason for being uninsured.',supported:true,why:'61.7% is the largest share reported.'},{text:'KFF: In 2024, 38.6% of uninsured adults delayed or skipped care due to cost, compared with 17.0% of privately insured adults.',claim:'Insured adults never delay care because of cost.',supported:false,why:'17.0% of privately insured adults also delayed or skipped care.'}])]],
['Medicaid expansion and the ACA',[
'The **Affordable Care Act (ACA)** of 2010 expanded coverage through marketplace subsidies, protections for people with preexisting conditions and an expansion of **Medicaid** to adults with incomes up to 138% of the poverty level. A 2012 Supreme Court decision made Medicaid expansion optional for states. KFF reports that in 2024 the uninsured rate was 14.5% in states that had not expanded Medicaid compared with 8.0% in expansion states, and that ten non-expansion states held 42% of the uninsured under age 65. Coverage fell in 2023–2024 as states ended pandemic-era continuous Medicaid enrollment and reviewed eligibility, a process often called “unwinding.”',
match('Match the coverage term',[['2010 law expanding coverage','Affordable Care Act'],['Public coverage for many low-income people','Medicaid'],['Public coverage for people 65 and older','Medicare'],['Coverage program for children in families above Medicaid limits','CHIP'],['Making Medicaid expansion optional for states','2012 Supreme Court decision'],['Eligibility reviews after continuous enrollment ended','Medicaid unwinding']])]],
['Beyond insurance: access and quality',[
'Insurance is necessary but not sufficient for access. People also need a usual source of care, clinicians who accept their insurance, transportation, time off work, language access and health literacy. **Underinsurance**—coverage with high deductibles or limited benefits—can still leave people unable to afford care. Quality matters too: hospitals serving low-income communities may have fewer resources. A full analysis of access considers availability, affordability, acceptability and accommodation.',
order('Steps people must complete to receive care',['Recognize a need for care','Have coverage or the ability to pay','Find a clinician who is available and accepts their coverage','Get transportation and time off to attend','Communicate in a language they understand','Afford follow-up tests and prescriptions'])]],
['Education, health literacy and medical debt',[
"Education shapes health in several ways. People with more years of schooling tend to have higher incomes, safer jobs, greater health literacy and more social resources, and they live longer on average. Education also influences the next generation: parental education is linked to children’s health. Schools themselves are health settings, offering meals, physical activity, vision and hearing screening and, in some places, school-based health centers. Disparities in school funding, often tied to local property taxes and residential segregation, can therefore become health disparities.",
"**Health literacy**—the capacity to find, understand and use information to make health decisions—varies across all education levels, and limited health literacy is associated with more hospitalizations and less use of preventive services. Health systems reduce the burden by using plain language, teach-back, pictures and simple forms, rather than expecting patients to decode complex instructions. Health literacy is one of Healthy People 2030’s overarching goals.",
"**Medical debt** is a distinct economic burden. KFF reports that in 2024, 62% of uninsured adults had health care debt compared with 44% of insured adults, showing that insurance reduces but does not eliminate financial strain. Debt can lead people to avoid future care, damage credit and housing options and increase stress, linking health care financing back to the social determinants of health.",
{t:'table',caption:"How socioeconomic factors affect health",head:["Factor","Pathways to health"],rows:[["Income","Food, housing, safe neighborhoods, ability to pay for care, stress"],["Education","Employment, health literacy, social networks, sense of control"],["Occupation","Workplace hazards, paid sick leave, schedules, employer insurance"],["Wealth and debt","Buffer against emergencies, stress, ability to seek care"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Health improves at every step of the income and education gradient.','KFF: 26.7 million nonelderly people (9.8%) were uninsured in 2024; most cite cost.','Medicaid non-expansion states had 14.5% uninsured versus 8.0% in expansion states.'],terms:[['Socioeconomic gradient','Stepwise improvement in health with higher income or education.'],['Medicaid expansion','ACA option extending Medicaid to adults up to 138% of poverty.'],['Underinsurance','Coverage that leaves people unable to afford needed care.']],mcqs:[q('According to KFF, how many nonelderly people were uninsured in the U.S. in 2024?','26.7 million','2.6 million','67 million','100 million','The uninsured rate was 9.8%.'),q('The most common reason uninsured adults gave for lacking coverage (KFF 2024) was:','Coverage was not affordable','They did not want coverage','They were too young','Their doctor refused them','61.7% cited affordability.'),q('Which group had the highest uninsured rate in KFF’s 2024 data?','American Indian or Alaska Native people','White people','Asian people','Children','The AIAN rate was 18.9%.'),q('In 2024, the uninsured rate in Medicaid non-expansion states was:','14.5%','8.0%','2%','30%','Expansion states had 8.0%.'),q('The socioeconomic gradient means:','Health improves at each step up the income or education ladder','Only the poorest have worse health','Wealth has no effect on health','Education harms health','The relationship is stepwise.'),q('Medicaid expansion under the ACA covers adults with incomes up to:','138% of the federal poverty level','50% of poverty','400% of poverty','Any income','This is the ACA expansion threshold.'),q('Which made Medicaid expansion optional for states?','A 2012 Supreme Court decision','HIPAA','EMTALA','The Belmont Report','States could choose to expand.'),q('Underinsurance means:','Having coverage that still leaves care unaffordable','Having no insurance','Having two insurance plans','Being enrolled in Medicare','High deductibles can block care.')],frq:{prompt:'Using KFF data, describe who is uninsured in the United States and why, explain the consequences of being uninsured, and evaluate how Medicaid expansion relates to coverage gaps.',rubric:[['Who is uninsured with data',['26.7','9.8','hispanic','american indian','poverty']],['Reasons uninsured',['afford','61.7','eligible','employer']],['Consequences',['delay','skip','debt','doctor','38.6']],['Medicaid expansion evaluation',['expansion','14.5','8.0','aca','138']]],model:'KFF reports that 26.7 million people under 65, or 9.8%, were uninsured in 2024, with the highest rates among American Indian or Alaska Native (18.9%) and Hispanic (18.4%) people and among people with incomes below twice the poverty level. Most uninsured adults said coverage was unaffordable (61.7%), others said they were ineligible, and many uninsured workers had employers that did not offer coverage. Uninsured adults were far more likely to delay or skip care because of cost (38.6% versus 17.0% of privately insured) and to carry medical debt. Medicaid expansion under the ACA covers adults up to 138% of poverty; the uninsured rate was 14.5% in non-expansion states versus 8.0% in expansion states, suggesting expansion is associated with smaller coverage gaps, though states differ in other ways too.'},sources:[RULES,KFF_UNINS,HP_SDOH]};

export const W5={title:'Geography: rural health and global inequities',hook:'A heart attack in a large city may be treated within an hour at a hospital with a catheterization lab. The same heart attack in a remote rural county may mean a long drive to a small hospital and a transfer. Across the world, the place a child is born predicts their chance of reaching age five. Geography is one of the strongest predictors of health.',objectives:['Describe rural–urban health disparities in the United States.','Explain global health inequities between low- and high-income countries.','Describe the role of primary health care and universal health coverage.','Interpret WHO estimates about primary health care.'],sections:[
['Rural health in the United States',[
'Depending on the definition used, roughly 14% to 20% of Americans live in rural areas. The CDC reports that rural residents are more likely than urban residents to die from leading causes such as heart disease, cancer, unintentional injury, chronic lower respiratory disease and stroke, and that rural areas tend to have older, lower-income populations with higher rates of smoking and less access to care. Rural communities face shortages of primary care clinicians and specialists, long travel distances, limited public transportation, closures of rural hospitals and maternity units, and gaps in broadband internet that limit telehealth. These challenges overlap with other disparities: many rural areas have large American Indian, Black or Hispanic populations.',
'Rural strengths also matter: close-knit communities, local knowledge and community health workers can support creative solutions. Programs such as Critical Access Hospitals, the National Health Service Corps (which supports clinicians in shortage areas), telehealth and community paramedicine aim to close gaps.',
sort('Barrier or strategy for rural health?',['Barrier','Strategy'],[['Closure of a rural hospital’s labor and delivery unit',0],['Long travel distance to specialists',0],['Limited broadband internet',0],['Telehealth visits with specialists',1],['Loan repayment for clinicians in shortage areas',1],['Community health workers visiting homes',1]])]],
['Global inequities',[
'Differences between countries are vast. WHO reports that life expectancy in low-income countries is about 16 years lower than in high-income countries, and that under-5 mortality among children from the poorest households is about double that among the richest. Leading causes of death differ: in low-income countries, infectious diseases, neonatal conditions and malnutrition still cause a large share of deaths, while high-income countries see more deaths from heart disease, cancer and dementia. Many middle-income countries face a **double burden** of continuing infectious disease and rising chronic disease.',
'Global inequities have the same roots as domestic ones—income, education, clean water and sanitation, food security, health workforce and political stability—operating at a larger scale. Our World in Data shows that child mortality and life expectancy have improved in nearly every country over decades, demonstrating that inequities are changeable.',
match('Match the global health term',[['Countries with lower national income per person','Low- and middle-income countries'],['Coexistence of infectious and chronic disease burdens','Double burden of disease'],['Essential care that is accessible close to where people live','Primary health care'],['Everyone can get needed care without financial hardship','Universal health coverage'],['2015 global goals including health targets','Sustainable Development Goals']])]],
['Primary health care and universal coverage',[
'**Primary health care (PHC)** is a whole-of-society approach to health centered on people’s needs, from health promotion and prevention to treatment, rehabilitation and palliative care, as close as possible to where people live. The 1978 Declaration of Alma-Ata and the 2018 Declaration of Astana affirmed PHC as the foundation of health systems. WHO’s primary health care fact sheet states that scaling up PHC interventions across low- and middle-income countries could save 60 million lives and increase average life expectancy by 3.7 years by 2030. WHO also estimates that most of the health gains targeted by the Sustainable Development Goals could be achieved through PHC.',
'**Universal health coverage (UHC)** means that all people can obtain the health services they need without financial hardship. It is Sustainable Development Goal target 3.8. Paying out of pocket for care pushes millions of families into poverty each year, so financial protection is part of health equity.',
bench('Is the claim supported by the WHO fact sheet?',[{text:'WHO: scaling up primary health care interventions across low- and middle-income countries could save 60 million lives and increase average life expectancy by 3.7 years by 2030.',claim:'According to WHO, expanding primary health care in low- and middle-income countries could add about 3.7 years to average life expectancy by 2030.',supported:true,why:'This restates WHO’s projection accurately, as an estimate rather than an observed result.'},{text:'WHO: life expectancy in low-income countries is about 16 years lower than in high-income countries.',claim:'WHO reports that life expectancy is the same in rich and poor countries.',supported:false,why:'WHO reports a gap of about 16 years.'}])]],
['Interpreting projections',[
'Statements such as “could save 60 million lives” are projections from models based on assumptions about coverage and effectiveness. They are useful for setting priorities, but they are not observed outcomes. When you read a projection, note the time frame, the conditions assumed and the source. On the HOSA test, questions may ask for the specific number reported by a source, such as WHO’s 3.7-year estimate, so record figures precisely along with their context.',
order('Evaluate a global health statistic',['Identify the source and publication date','Determine whether it is an observed measure or a projection','Note the population, place and time frame','Check the unit and comparison group','Consider what assumptions or data limits apply','State the figure precisely with its context'])]],
['Connecting rural and global',[
'Rural U.S. communities and low-income countries share challenges—distance, workforce shortages and limited infrastructure—and some shared solutions, such as community health workers, task-shifting to trained nonphysician staff and telehealth. Comparing them helps you recognize that geography interacts with income, race and policy. In essay questions, use specific examples from WHO, CDC or KFF and connect them to the determinants of health.']],
['Water, sanitation, nutrition and climate',[
"Some of the largest global inequities come from basic living conditions. Lack of safe drinking water, sanitation and hygiene contributes to diarrheal disease, one of the leading causes of death among young children in low-income countries. Undernutrition weakens immunity, contributes to child deaths and causes **stunting** (low height for age), which affects development and learning. At the same time, many low- and middle-income countries are seeing rising rates of overweight and diet-related chronic disease, part of the double burden. Vaccination coverage differs across and within countries, and gaps leave children vulnerable to measles and other preventable infections.",
"**Climate change** is increasingly recognized as a health equity issue. Heat waves, floods, droughts, air pollution and shifts in infectious disease patterns affect everyone, but low-income countries and marginalized communities within wealthy countries often face the greatest exposure and have the fewest resources to adapt. Within cities, neighborhoods with fewer trees and more pavement—often lower-income neighborhoods—can be several degrees hotter during heat waves. Addressing these determinants requires investment beyond hospitals: water systems, food programs, housing and climate adaptation.",
"When comparing countries, remember that national averages hide internal inequities. A middle-income country may have modern urban hospitals alongside rural areas with no clinic, and a high-income country may have communities with life expectancy similar to much poorer nations. Geography operates at every scale, from continents to city blocks."]]
],takeaways:['Rural Americans have higher death rates from leading causes and face workforce and distance barriers.','WHO: life expectancy in low-income countries is about 16 years lower than in high-income countries.','WHO projects that scaling up primary health care in LMICs could add 3.7 years to life expectancy and save 60 million lives by 2030.'],terms:[['Primary health care','Whole-of-society, people-centered essential care close to where people live.'],['Universal health coverage','Access to needed services without financial hardship (SDG 3.8).'],['Double burden of disease','Coexisting infectious and chronic disease burdens.']],mcqs:[q('According to WHO, how many years could be added to average life expectancy if primary care interventions were scaled up in low- and middle-income countries?','3.7 years','2.4 years','2.7 years','3.4 years','WHO projects 3.7 years by 2030.'),q('WHO estimates scaling up PHC in LMICs could save about how many lives by 2030?','60 million','6 million','600,000','1 billion','The fact sheet cites 60 million lives.'),q('Universal health coverage is Sustainable Development Goal target:','3.8','1.1','5.5','10.2','UHC is target 3.8.'),q('Which document first affirmed primary health care as the foundation of health for all in 1978?','Declaration of Alma-Ata','Belmont Report','Declaration of Helsinki','Nuremberg Code','Alma-Ata, 1978; reaffirmed in Astana, 2018.'),q('A barrier commonly faced by rural communities is:','Closure of hospitals and maternity units','Too many specialists','Excess broadband access','Short travel distances','Rural closures limit access.'),q('The double burden of disease refers to:','Coexisting infectious and chronic disease burdens','Two diseases in one patient','Paying twice for care','Disease in two countries','Many middle-income countries face both.'),q('Which statement about WHO’s “60 million lives” figure is most accurate?','It is a projection based on assumptions','It is an observed count from 2020','It describes high-income countries','It is a historical total','Projections are model estimates.'),q('Under-5 mortality among the poorest households compared with the richest, according to WHO, is about:','Double','The same','Half','Ten times','WHO reports roughly double.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare health challenges in rural U.S. communities with those in low-income countries, describe WHO’s case for primary health care including its 3.7-year projection, and explain how you would evaluate such a projection.',rubric:[['Rural U.S. challenges',['rural','distance','workforce','hospital','closure','cdc']],['Global inequities with data',['16 years','low-income','under-5','double']],['Primary health care and projection',['primary health care','3.7','60 million','2030','who']],['Evaluating projections',['projection','assumption','model','time frame','source']]],model:'Rural U.S. communities face higher death rates from leading causes, shortages of clinicians, long travel distances, hospital and maternity unit closures and limited broadband, while low-income countries face life expectancy about 16 years lower than high-income countries and child mortality among the poorest households about double that of the richest. Both share workforce and distance barriers, and both benefit from community health workers and telehealth. WHO’s primary health care fact sheet states that scaling up primary health care in low- and middle-income countries could save 60 million lives and increase average life expectancy by 3.7 years by 2030. I would treat this as a model-based projection, noting the source, time frame, assumptions about coverage and effectiveness, and that it is not an observed result.'},sources:[RULES,WHO_PHC,WHO_EQ,CDC_RURAL]};
